critiques.description:
The regularity reading in Section 2.8 and in conclusions 2.10.4 and 'What we have learned' says bans 'act more like an instruction to do something other than the obvious', 'violate regularity' and 'displace the internal state'. This reading is underdetermined. Every violating confirmation ban names an in-domain algorithm:
- banning greedy best-first moves A* from 3% to 58%;
- banning Bellman-Ford moves Dijkstra from 5% to 56%;
- banning radix, counting or shell sort lowers bubble sort.
A plain semantic-priming account predicts exactly these shifts: naming an algorithm raises its close relatives (A* is greedy best-first plus path cost; Dijkstra is Bellman-Ford's sibling). The run's own mention controls show that naming is extremely potent ('You may use A*' gives 95% A*). The placebo (Rabin-Karp, an out-of-domain name) and the cross-domain ban control only for 'a prohibition sentence exists', not for 'an in-domain name is mentioned'. The logit-lens 'no priming' check looks only at the banned name's own rank, not its neighbours'. Under a priming account the regularity 'violation' is a context change, not a choice-set result, so it does not bear on the random-utility framing at all.

dimension_scores.justification:
The report now preserves essentially all executed work across four artifacts: more than 50k programs and every table on disk that bears on the claims. A few result objects are on disk but absent:
- the KNOCKOUT_PLACEBO_SENT cells (transplant.json);
- per-layer probe-band curves (exp6 qwen_probe_band.json, gemma_probe_band.json; exp5 band_selection.json), summarised in one sentence;
- the UMAP/LDA manifold variants and the T4 manifold coordinates (exp5 figures);
- the exp6 centroid map (fig_centroid_map).
The reasoning for each step and the dead ends are well kept.
